/Title (Webb was quiet for a long moment, longer than any pause he'd allowed himself so far. "I regret that I didn't trust the room enough to ask first," he said finally. "I keep turning that over, more than the launch itself. I could have brought the contingency to the full Council, argued for it openly, let all of you tell me I was wrong before it ever became something irreversible. I didn't, because some part of me already suspected you'd say no, and I decided in advance that my judgment mattered more than that no would have. That's the actual failure. Not the fear. The certainty that my fear was worth more than everyone else's consent.")
